course: dictionary
lesson: 15315
title: Small words for small amounts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how much of something there is. «A smidge» is one of them. It is an informal word, the kind you hear in everyday talk, and it means a very small amount. «Can I have a smidge of butter on my toast?» asks for just a tiny bit of butter.
explain: Look at the words around it to work out the size. «The soup was a smidge too hot to eat.» The word too tells you the soup was only slightly hotter than it should be, so a smidge means a little bit. Ask yourself: would a big amount fit here? If not, the word is telling you the amount is small.
explain: Other words that mean about the same are smidgen, tad, touch, pinch and a bit. Some people say «a smidge» as a joke, or to sound modest, even when the thing is not really small. The context tells you which meaning the speaker wants.
fact: A smidge is an informal word, so you hear it in chatty talk more than in formal writing.
fact: «A smidge» means a very small amount of something, such as «a smidge of butter».
fact: «A smidge» can also mean slightly, as in «a smidge too long».
fact: Smidgen, tad, touch, pinch and a bit are related words with a similar meaning.
fact: People sometimes say «a smidge» as a joke or to sound modest, even when the thing is quite big.
exercise: «She put a smidge of sugar in her tea.» What does a smidge of sugar mean? | A huge bag of it | *A very small amount | A full cup of it | A lot, all at once
exercise: «The film was a smidge too long for me.» What does a smidge too long mean? | Far too long to bear | Exactly the right length | *Slightly too long | Very short indeed
exercise: Which word means nearly the same as smidge? | Gigantic | Enormous | Loud | *Tad
test: «Can you leave a smidge of cake for me?» Does the speaker want a big slice or a tiny bit? | Nothing at all | A whole cake | A big slice | *A tiny bit
test: Jake says, «This is a smidge too big to fit.» What does the smidge show? | *It is only slightly too big | It is made of metal | It is far too small | It is the perfect size
exam: Ava says, «I have a smidge of homework left», but she has three pages to do. Why might she say smidge? | *She is joking, or sounding modest about it | She means she has no homework at all | She means she has lost her pencil | She means three pages is tiny
